WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:13.500
It is half past one. Galih has an interview on Friday and a thesis chapter due, and for a week he has slept five hours a night.

2
00:00:13.500 --> 00:00:22.500
The trouble is what a tired brain loses first: attention, working memory and control of mood. Exactly the skills an interview tests.

3
00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:31.400
Usable energy comes from four systems. Sleep: it stores what you studied and restores the self-control you interview with.

4
00:00:31.400 --> 00:00:37.100
Movement: thirty brisk minutes on most days lifts mood and focus.

5
00:00:37.100 --> 00:00:42.800
Fuel: regular meals with protein, and coffee early in the day.

6
00:00:42.800 --> 00:00:49.700
And recovery: a real off-switch with people, nature or play. Scrolling does not count.

7
00:00:49.700 --> 00:00:57.000
Galih has made a health plan before. It was perfect, and it lasted two days.

8
00:00:57.000 --> 00:01:03.100
A plan built for your best week breaks in your worst one.

9
00:01:03.100 --> 00:01:11.200
So this time he writes a floor: four lines small enough to keep in a bad week.

10
00:01:11.200 --> 00:01:19.700
In a crunch week the floor may shrink: six hours, one walk. But it never goes to zero.

11
00:01:19.700 --> 00:01:28.600
Capacity grows the way a muscle does: a demand that stretches you a little, then enough rest to adapt.

12
00:01:28.600 --> 00:01:33.900
The growth happens in the rest, not in the effort.

13
00:01:33.900 --> 00:01:40.000
Take the rest away, and the same effort only wears you down.

14
00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:49.000
Brad Stulberg and Steve Magness put it as an equation: stress plus rest equals growth. So plan recovery as deliberately as the work.

15
00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:55.900
Overload sends early signals, weeks before real trouble. Sleep that no longer refreshes you.

16
00:01:55.900 --> 00:01:58.900
Irritation at small things.

17
00:01:58.900 --> 00:02:03.400
And pulling away from people you normally enjoy.

18
00:02:03.400 --> 00:02:12.400
The response has two steps. Subtract first: drop one commitment for two weeks and protect your sleep. Then talk, to a friend, a mentor or a counsellor.

19
00:02:12.400 --> 00:02:21.400
And if a low mood stays most days for two weeks, seeing a professional is the competent move, as it would be for a broken bone.

20
00:02:21.400 --> 00:02:30.400
Friday morning. Four nights inside his sleep window, and a walk to campus. The interview is the same. The brain he brings to it is not.

21
00:02:30.400 --> 00:02:39.400
And on a day that feels harder than it should, he checks his levers before he blames his character: sleep, heat, food, mental load.

22
00:02:39.400 --> 00:02:45.100
A floor you never break beats a ceiling you never reach.

23
00:02:45.100 --> 00:02:49.200
Capacity grows in the rest after effort.

24
00:02:49.200 --> 00:02:57.700
Now it is your turn. Write your four lines: a sleep window, movement, one real meal, one person.
